<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/foundational/walk-through-western-art">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Art history is not a parade of genius portraits — it is the record of what different eras
    needed images to do: teach the illiterate, glorify patrons, see accurately, express the
    inner life, question the market itself. Follow eleven works and the Western tradition
    outlines itself: <em>what changed, and what changed it</em>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="six centuries, eleven works — scrub through the shifts" widget="timeline" config={artTimeline} />

  <h2>The engines behind the shifts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Technology:</strong> oil paint (1400s) allowed blending and revision; cheap pigments in tubes (1841) dragged painting outdoors; photography (1839) freed painting from accuracy and pushed it toward impression and abstraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Patrons:</strong> church → court → merchant → museum → market. Every style above is also an economic story about who paid and why.</li>
    <li><strong>Ideas:</strong> humanism elevated the individual; the Enlightenment demanded observation; industrialisation and war broke confidence in the visible world — hence abstraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Feedback:</strong> each generation defines itself against the last. "Modern" art is partly a 150-year conversation about what to do once photography took likeness away.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to look at anything here</h2>
  <p>
    Ask four questions in order: <em>What do I literally see?</em> (value, colour, space)
    — <em>How was it made?</em> (medium and technique are ideas) — <em>Who was it for?</em>
    (function and patron) — <em>What did it change?</em> Only the first question has a short
    answer.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gombrich, The Story of Art; standard museum scholarship (Met, MoMA, Uffizi object histories).</p>
</TopicPage>
